# Suppressions must name the control and the reason. An entry without a reason
# is a finding someone hid, and review should treat it as one. Everything not
# listed here fails the build.

# AVD-AWS-0053 — the application load balancer is internet-facing by design.
# Exposure is bounded by access-control's alb_ingress_cidrs, which policy
# IAM-02 asserts on and which the landing zone publishes as evidence.
AVD-AWS-0053

# AVD-AZU-0047 — the Azure web tier NSG accepts 80/443 from the internet for
# the same reason and in the same position as the AWS load balancer above.
# Only those two ports are open; rule 4096 denies everything else inbound.
AVD-AZU-0047

# AVD-AWS-0104 — instances need outbound internet for OS patching and agent
# registration, and reach it through the NAT gateway. Egress is already
# restricted to tcp/80 and tcp/443; the destination cannot be narrowed further
# without pinning mirror and API addresses that the providers do not publish.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AVD-AWS-0132 — buckets are encrypted with SSE-KMS under the AWS-managed key.
# A customer-managed key is the stricter option, but it needs a key policy
# granting the ELB log-delivery and CloudWatch Logs service principals, which
# has to be validated against a live account before it ships. Tracked, not
# dismissed: until then the data is encrypted, just not under our own key.
AVD-AWS-0132
